## Why This Is Asked
IBM wants to see how you handle change management, influence without authority, and drive technical decisions through data and collaboration rather than just technical expertise.

## Expected Answer
A strong candidate will describe: 1) A specific technical challenge or opportunity, 2) How they gathered data/evidence to support their proposal, 3) How they addressed team concerns through demos or proof-of-concepts, 4) The measurable business impact of the change.

## Code Example
```typescript
// Example: Converting team to TypeScript
interface LegacyAPI {
  data: any;
  status: string;
}

// Improved TypeScript version
interface ModernAPI<T> {
  data: T;
  status: 'success' | 'error' | 'pending';
}

// Proof of concept showing type safety
function processAPI<T>(response: ModernAPI<T>): T | null {
  return response.status === 'success' ? response.data : null;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses incident triage, cross-functional coordination, regulatory risk awareness, and durable fixes under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-team triage and decision rights
- Measurable recovery targets (MTTR, availability)
- Canary deployments, feature flags, rollback plans
- Postmortem with automation to prevent recurrence

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple canary flag check
const isCanary = featureFlags.canary && region === 'eu-west';
if (isCanary) {
  route = '/api/v2/canary';
} else {
  route = '/api/v2';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- What metrics did you monitor during the remediation?
- How did you communicate progress to leadership and stakeholders?
- What long-term safeguards did you implement to prevent recurrence?

